 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Start with the primes: two, three, five, seven, eleven,",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 4.287
  },
  {
   "text": "numbers with no divisors but one and themselves.",
   "start": 4.287,
   "end": 7.513
  },
  {
   "text": "Up close, they look random.",
   "start": 7.513,
   "end": 9.475
  },
  {
   "text": "But count them.",
   "start": 9.475,
   "end": 10.688
  },
  {
   "text": "π(x) is the number of primes up to x.",
   "start": 10.688,
   "end": 12.787
  },
  {
   "text": "Its graph is a staircase, and from far away the staircase looks astonishingly",
   "start": 12.787,
   "end": 17.162
  },
  {
   "text": "smooth.",
   "start": 17.162,
   "end": 18.075
  },
  {
   "text": "As a teenager, Gauss guessed that near x, roughly one number in ln x is prime.",
   "start": 18.975,
   "end": 24.213
  },
  {
   "text": "Adding up those odds gives the logarithmic integral,",
   "start": 24.213,
   "end": 27.213
  },
  {
   "text": "li(x).",
   "start": 27.213,
   "end": 27.975
  },
  {
   "text": "Up to a million there are 78,498 primes,",
   "start": 27.975,
   "end": 32.75
  },
  {
   "text": "and L I is off by only about a hundred and thirty.",
   "start": 32.75,
   "end": 36.013
  },
  {
   "text": "The real question is: how big can that error get?",
   "start": 36.013,
   "end": 39.425
  }
 ],
